{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00987 - MONDO_grouped_18874_4643",
  "verbalization": "Cytarabine, a nucleoside analog, is strongly associated with the treatment of myeloid leukemia through multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Cytarabine is indicated for both Acute Myeloblastic Leukemia With Maturation and Without Maturation, with Quizartinib as a common indication in both cases. Quizartinib, a tyrosine kinase inhibitor, is linked to myeloid leukemia, indicating a potential mechanism of action that may be shared between Cytarabine and Quizartinib. The presence of a common pathway involving Quizartinib and myeloid leukemia suggests that Cytarabine may exert a similar effect, possibly through a mechanism involving the inhibition of certain cellular processes that are dysregulated in myeloid leukemia. The integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that Cytarabine may have a role in the treatment of myeloid leukemia, although further mechanistic studies are needed to confirm the exact mechanism of action.",
